            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "*2 additional call site(s) collapsed during dedup \u2014 same root cause; each location needs the same fix applied.*\n\n#### Description\nThe POST /auth/login handler at api/src/routes/auth.js:37 applies no rate-limit middleware before reaching the bcrypt.compare call at line 44. The route is registered directly on the Express router with only asyncHandler wrapping. No express-rate-limit, express-brute, or account-lockout counter is present anywhere in this route or visible in the neighbor context. The same applies to POST /auth/register (line 11), which an attacker could hammer to enumerate the taken-email check at line 20\u201322 as a side channel for valid email discovery. An attacker with a list of email addresses (or just targeting the known default admin email from seeding) can spray passwords at arbitrary throughput from a single IP.\n\n#### Impact\nAn unauthenticated attacker can submit unlimited login attempts against any account with no server-side throttling, lockout, or CAPTCHA. Combined with credential-stuffing wordlists or targeted brute-force of the 8-character minimum password space, any account\u2014including admin accounts\u2014can be compromised without triggering any alert or block. bcrypt's computational cost (~50\u2013100 ms/attempt at cost=10) limits throughput to roughly 10\u201320 req/s per CPU core, but at scale across distributed IPs that is still thousands of guesses per minute.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker sends POST /api/auth/login with {\"email\":\"admin@widgetshop.example\",\"password\":\"<guess>\"} in a tight loop using a tool such as ffuf or Hydra. With bcrypt cost=10, approximately 10 guesses/second are achievable per attacking thread. An 8-character lowercase-alpha password space is 26^8 \u2248 200 billion; however, targeting the documented default 'ChangeMe123!' or a common wordlist of ~1 million passwords succeeds within minutes. No lock-out or CAPTCHA interrupts the attack. The attacker receives a JWT with role='admin' upon success.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Target account email address is known or guessable (e.g., admin@widgetshop.example from documentation or seed files)\n- Network access to port 8080 (public-facing nginx proxy)\n\n```\nrouter.post('/login', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {\n  const { email, password } = req.body || {};\n  if (!email || !password) {\n    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'email and password are required' });\n  }\n\n  const user = await db('users').where({ email }).first();\n  if (!user || !(await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password_hash))) {\n    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid email or password' });\n  }\n  ...\n}));\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nApply express-rate-limit (or equivalent) as middleware on the /auth/login and /auth/register routes, limiting to e.g. 5 attempts per IP per 15 minutes. Additionally implement account-level lockout (e.g. 10 failed attempts triggers a 15-minute lock stored in Redis/DB). In api/src/routes/auth.js, add the middleware before the router.post('/login', ...) call at line 37. Consider also adding a bcrypt dummy comparison when the user is not found (see timing finding) to prevent enumeration.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 9.1. Pre-auth, AC:L, no friction. No rate-limit, lockout, or CAPTCHA on POST /auth/login. The seeded admin and CS-agent accounts use the publicly documented default password 'ChangeMe123!', making exploitation trivial without any brute-force at all. For other accounts the unlimited spray throughput enables credential-stuffing at wire speed. Directly unlocks every privileged role and is the entry point for the refund-fraud and PII-dump chains.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 Route confirmed with no rate-limiting at any layer (app, proxy, or nginx); known seeded admin email+password makes exploitation trivial; full admin JWT is the reward\n\nI now have all the evidence needed for a determination.\n\n**Summary of findings:**\n\n1. **Route (lines 37\u201353, confirmed):** `router.post('/login', asyncHandler(...))` \u2014 onl\u2026",
