        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-321",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hardcoded fallback API key bypasses authentication when env var unset  [CVSS 8.2: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:H/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "fauxpay/src/server.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "8.2",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-321",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:H/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-321",
            "cweId": "CWE-321",
            "cvssScore": 8.2,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.9,
            "description": "#### Description\nLine 4 reads `const API_KEY = [REDACTED-SECRET] || 'fauxpay_test_key'`. The right-hand operand of `||` is the literal string `'fauxpay_test_key'`. If the referenced environment variable evaluates to an empty string, undefined, or is simply absent from the container's environment, JavaScript's short-circuit evaluation selects the hardcoded fallback. The `requireApiKey` middleware (line 17) then grants access to any caller presenting `[REDACTED-BEARER]`. Because this is a publicly distributed training/demo application, the source is expected to be read by a broad audience, making the fallback value effectively public knowledge. The `/charge` endpoint (line 43) uses this guard to control who may authorise financial transactions and create transaction records that can later be used by `/refund`.\n\n#### Impact\nIf the container starts without the expected environment variable set, the API key falls back to the publicly-visible string `fauxpay_test_key`. Anyone who has read the source code (a training/demo repository likely to be public) can immediately authenticate to /charge and /refund without any brute-force.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nA developer deploys the Docker Compose stack without setting the FauxPay API key environment variable (e.g., when running from a fresh clone with no `.env` file). An attacker who has read the GitHub repository sends `POST /fauxpay/charge` with `Authorization: [REDACTED-BEARER]` and any card token obtained from the unauthenticated /tokenize endpoint, successfully recording a fraudulent transaction.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- The FauxPay container is started without the API key environment variable set (no .env file, misconfigured deployment, or CI pipeline that doesn't inject secrets)\n- Attacker has network access to port 8080 \u2014 no credentials or prior access required\n\n```\nconst API_KEY = [REDACTED-SECRET] || 'fauxpay_test_key';\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nRemove the hardcoded fallback entirely. On startup, check that the variable is set and non-empty and abort with a fatal error if it is not: `const API_KEY = [REDACTED-SECRET]; if (!API_KEY) { console.error('FATAL: FAUXPAY_API_KEY must be set'); process.exit(1); }`. This makes misconfiguration fail loudly at deploy time rather than silently degrading to a known-public credential.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 8.2. Pre-auth, AC:L. The hardcoded fallback 'fauxpay_test_key' is readable in source and activates when FAUXPAY_API_KEY is absent or empty \u2014 the common state for a sample/dev deployment. nginx routes /fauxpay/* from port 8080, making /fauxpay/charge and /fauxpay/refund internet-reachable. Standalone high; anchors a critical pre-auth financial fraud chain with finding 15 (token never invalidated).\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 Hardcoded fallback `fauxpay_test_key` matches the committed docker-compose default and `.env.example` value; nginx proxies `/fauxpay/` with no auth gate; port 8080 is publicly exposed; `/tokenize` is unauthenticated, giving any attacker everything needed to call `/charge` or `/refund` without credentials.\n\nI have now read all relevant files. Let me consolidate the findings:\n\n**Evidence gathered:**\n\n1. **`fauxpay/src/server.js` line 4** \u2014 confirmed: `const API_KEY = [REDACTED-SECRET] || 'fauxpay_test_key';`\n2. **`requireApiKey` middleware (lines 14\u201319)** \u2014 checks `Authorization: Bearer <key>` against `API_KEY`. If `FAUXPAY_API_KEY` is unset, every caller presenting `[REDACTED-BEARER]` passes.\n3. **`/charge` (line 43) and `/refund` (line 62)** \u2014 both gated by `requireApiKey`. `/tokenize` (line 29) is entirely open.\n4. **`docker-compose.yml` line 55** \u2014 `FAUXPAY_API_KEY: ${FAUXPAY_API_KEY:-fauxpay_test_key}` \u2014 compose-level default is *also* `fauxpay_test_key`, providing zero lift over the code fallback.\n5. **`.env.example` line 12** \u2014 `FAUXPAY_API_KEY=fauxpay_test_key` \u2014 the documented \"start here\" value IS the fallback literal. Copy `.env.example \u2192 .env` (the standard first-run \u2026"
          },
