          "ruleId": "CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Base image pulled by mutable tag, no digest pin  [CVSS 8.3: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "api/Dockerfile"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "8.3",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H",
            "cwe": "CWE-829 - Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cweId": "CWE-829",
            "cweName": "Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere",
            "cvssScore": 8.3,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nBoth `FROM node:20-alpine AS deps` (line 1) and `FROM node:20-alpine AS runtime` (line 6) resolve the tag at build time against whatever digest Docker Hub currently points `node:20-alpine` at. There is no `@sha256:<digest>` pin, so two builds on different days may pull different images with different contents. Docker does not verify the image's content against a known-good hash. Supply-chain attacks against public base images (tag overwrite, mirror poisoning, compromised upstream publisher) are well-documented; unpinned tags are the entry point for all of them.\n\n#### Impact\nIf the upstream `node:20-alpine` tag is replaced (Docker Hub compromise, tag mutation, or cache-poisoning), every subsequent `docker build` silently pulls and executes the attacker-controlled image. Both the `deps` and `runtime` stages are affected, meaning the final runtime image is fully under attacker control.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker compromises the Docker Hub `library/node` account or a mirroring registry used by the CI builder. They push a backdoored `node:20-alpine` image. The next scheduled or manual `docker build` of `api/Dockerfile` silently installs the attacker's image. The resulting runtime container ships a backdoor reachable on the deployed port 3000, giving the attacker persistent access inside the container network where the PostgreSQL port 5432 is directly reachable.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Upstream node:20-alpine tag on Docker Hub (or a mirror) must be modified by an attacker\n- A new build of api/Dockerfile must be triggered after the tag is mutated\n\n```\nFROM node:20-alpine AS deps\n...\nFROM node:20-alpine AS runtime\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nPin both FROM lines to an immutable digest: `FROM node:20-alpine@sha256:<current-digest> AS deps` and the same for the runtime stage. Obtain the digest with `docker buildx imagetools inspect node:20-alpine` or from Docker Hub's digest column. Automate digest rotation via Dependabot or Renovate with a reviewer approval gate to preserve auditability.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 8.3. Pre-auth but AC:H + UI:R; requires supply-chain compromise or CI/CD pipeline manipulation. Both FROM node:20-alpine stages lack digest pins. A poisoned upstream tag causes malicious code to run in the api container on the next image build. Primary chain value: code execution in the api container unlocks the trivial DB pivot via finding 7.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 7/10) \u2014 Both `FROM node:20-alpine` lines confirmed unpinned; no digest, no Renovate/Dependabot, no mirror isolation; malicious base image lands arbitrary code in container with full DB credential access\n\n**Analysis**\n\n**Step B \u2014 Confirming the code:**\nThe `api/Dockerfile` is exactly as reported:\n- Line 1: `FROM node:20-alpine AS deps`\n- Line 6: `FROM node:20-alpine AS runtime`\n\nBoth `FROM` instructions use a mutable tag with no `@sha256:<digest>` pin. Additionally, `docker-compose.yml` builds this image (`build: ./api`) \u2014 so a `docker-compose build` or `docker-compose up --build` will re-pull whatever digest `node:20-alpine` points to at build time.\n\n**Step C \u2014 Walking the chain:**\n- `docker-compose.yml` confirms the `api` service is always built from this Dockerfile (not pulled as a pre-built image).\n- The API container is the only service with access to the database host (`DB_HOST: db`, port 5432), env-file credentials, and payment service credentials (`FAUXPAY_BASE_URL`). A backdoored base image runs with all of these.\n- No GitHub Actions / CI workflows exist in the repo \u2014 no pipeline to verify digests before or after build.\n\n**Step D \u2014 Looking for defences:**\n- **Digest pin**: None \u2014 confirmed by `Grep sha256 Dockerfile*` returning zero results across all three Dockerfiles.\n- **Automated rotation tooling**: No Renovate config, no Dependabot config anywhere.\n- **Content trust / `DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST`**: No configuration found.\n- **Mirror / private registry**: `docker-compose.yml` pulls directly from \u2026"
          },
