            "description": "#### Description\nIn `requireApiKey` (line 17), the extracted bearer token is compared to `API_KEY` with the JavaScript strict-inequality operator `!==`. V8 string comparison exits at the first differing byte, so a correct n-character prefix takes measurably longer to reject than an n-0 prefix. An attacker who submits many requests with a fixed guess prefix and varies the next character can identify the correct character at each position by measuring median response time. The crypto module that is already imported (line 2) exposes `crypto.timingSafeEqual`, which runs in constant time regardless of where the strings diverge. The untrusted input flows from: `req.headers.authorization` (line 15) \u2192 string slice (line 16) \u2192 `!==` comparison against the secret (line 17). nginx routes `/fauxpay/*` to this service, making both `/fauxpay/charge` and `/fauxpay/refund` reachable from port 8080.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker who can reach the /charge or /refund endpoints can recover the FauxPay API key one character at a time by measuring response latency differences. With the key in hand they can issue arbitrary charges and refunds against any stored transaction.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker sends POST /fauxpay/charge with `Authorization: Bearer X` where X is a 1-character candidate, repeats this 128 times per candidate across all printable ASCII values, and picks the character producing the highest average latency (indicating a match was found at position 0 before bailing out at position 1). They repeat for each subsequent character position until the full key is reconstructed, then POST a charge or refund with the recovered key.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Network access to port 8080 (the public nginx port) \u2014 no authentication required\n- Ability to send a statistically significant number of requests (several hundred per character position) and measure response times with sub-millisecond precision \u2014 achievable over a LAN or from inside the Docker Compose network\n\n```\nfunction requireApiKey(req, res, next) {\n  const header = req.headers.authorization || '';\n  const key = header.startsWith('Bearer ') ? header.slice(7) : null;\n  if (key !== API_KEY) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid FauxPay API key' });\n  next();\n}\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nReplace the `!==` comparison with a constant-time comparator. Both operands must be `Buffer` objects of equal length: `const supplied = Buffer.from(key || ''); const expected = Buffer.from(API_KEY); if (supplied.length !== expected.length || !crypto.timingSafeEqual(supplied, expected)) return res.status(401)...`. The length pre-check must itself not leak \u2014 pad or always compare against a fixed-length canonical form, or use a keyed HMAC tag comparison.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 7.4. Pre-auth, AC:H (requires many timed requests). Non-constant-time `!==` in requireApiKey leaks the real FauxPay API key character-by-character via response latency. Practically reachable from the internet via the /fauxpay/* nginx route. When the env var is set (overriding finding 10's fallback), this is the only remote path to the actual key. Together with finding 10 they guarantee key recovery regardless of deployment state.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 `!==` non-constant-time comparison of bearer token against secret confirmed at line 17, reachable via nginx `/fauxpay/` with no upstream auth gate; `crypto.timingSafeEqual` is imported but unused.\n\n**Verification complete.** Here is my analysis:\n\n**A. Code exactly matches the finding (lines 14\u201317):**\n- Line 2: `const crypto = require('crypto');` \u2014 crypto is imported but `timingSafeEqual` is never used.\n- Line 4: `const API_KEY = [REDACTED-SECRET] || 'fauxpay_test_key';` \u2014 hardcoded fallback, but a real secret can be injected.\n- Line 17: `if (key !== API_KEY)` \u2014 JavaScript `!==` is a short-circuit byte comparator; V8 bails on first mismatch.\n\n**B. Nginx routing confirmed (web/nginx.conf lines 12\u201315):**\n```\nlocation /fauxpay/ {\n\u2026"
