            "description": "#### Description\nIn `POST /api/orders`, stock is decremented for every cart line inside a `db.transaction()` block (lines 61-63) that commits successfully before the code reaches `fauxpay.charge()` (line 70). The charge call is outside the transaction. When it throws (line 71), the catch block only updates the order status to 'cancelled' (line 72) \u2014 it never rolls back or increments the stock_quantity back. The database is now permanently inconsistent: the order is cancelled but the stock is permanently reduced. This is reachable by any authenticated user who holds a valid JWT and has at least one saved shipping address.\n\n#### Impact\nAny authenticated customer can reduce any widget's stock_quantity to zero without a successful purchase, because stock is decremented inside a committed database transaction before the payment call, and never restored when the payment fails. Repeatedly triggering this drains all inventory for a target SKU, preventing legitimate customers from purchasing.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker authenticates as a customer and saves a shipping address. They add 100 units of Widget X (current stock = 100) to their cart and POST `/api/orders` with `shipping_address_id=<valid>` and `card_token=invalid_garbage`. The server commits the transaction (stock goes to 0), then calls FauxPay with the bad token; FauxPay rejects the charge, the catch block marks the order cancelled, and no stock is restored. Widget X now shows stock_quantity = 0 and cannot be purchased by anyone.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has a valid authenticated session (any customer role)\n- Attacker has at least one valid shipping address saved (trivially obtained via POST /api/users/me/addresses)\n\n```\nconst order = await db.transaction(async (trx) => {\n  // ... creates order and order_items ...\n  for (const li of lineItems) {\n    await trx('widgets').where({ id: li.widget_id }).decrement('stock_quantity', li.quantity);  // committed\n  }\n  return { id: orderId };\n});\n\nlet chargeResult;\ntry {\n  chargeResult = await fauxpay.charge({ cardToken: [REDACTED-SECRET], amountCents: totalCents, orderId: order.id });\n} catch (err) {\n  await db('orders').where({ id: order.id }).update({ status: 'cancelled' });  // stock NOT restored\n  return res.status(payErrorStatus(err)).json({ error: 'Payment failed', detail: err.data?.error });\n}\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nStock restoration must happen inside the same transaction as the decrement, or the decrement must be deferred until after payment succeeds. The cleanest fix: move `fauxpay.charge()` inside the `db.transaction()` block and roll back the entire transaction (including stock decrements) if the charge fails. Alternatively, keep the current order of operations but add `await db('widgets').where({ id: li.widget_id }).increment('stock_quantity', li.quantity)` inside the catch block alongside the order cancellation at `orders.js:72`.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 7.1. Authenticated (PR:L). The db.transaction() commits stock decrements before the out-of-transaction fauxpay.charge() call; a payment failure or induced timeout never restores inventory. Any authenticated user can intentionally fail payments to permanently drain product stock. Amplified by finding 12's concurrent oversell race, which can drive stock_quantity negative without a single successful purchase.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 transaction commits stock decrement before payment; catch block never restores stock; confirmed in actual source with no compensating defence anywhere in the handler or middleware stack\n\nI now have everything needed for a complete verdict. Here is my analysis:"
