            "description": "#### Description\nState-machine defect: FauxPay models tokens with only one state \u2014 'valid/present in the Map' \u2014 and has no 'consumed' or 'expired' transition.\n\n1. Untrusted input enters at POST /fauxpay/tokenize (line 29): any external caller, including the Checkout SPA, submits card_number, exp_month, exp_year, cvv.  No authentication is required on this endpoint.\n2. At line 39, `tokens.set(token, { last4, brand })` writes the new token into the in-memory Map with no TTL, no use-count, and no subsequent deletion path.\n3. At line 45 in POST /fauxpay/charge (API-key-protected), `const card = tokens.get(card_token)` reads the token back \u2014 but there is no `tokens.delete(card_token)` anywhere in the handler.  The token remains valid for an unlimited number of future /charge calls.\n\nTrust boundary crossed: the card_token is created by an unauthenticated external call (SPA or direct HTTP client); it then travels through the untrusted SPA tier back to the API backend as part of the checkout body; the API backend relays it to /charge.  Because the token is never consumed, a second checkout submission (by the same user or by a different principal who observed the token \u2014 e.g. via XSS or IDOR in the API layer) causes an additional charge against the same card without any re-verification gate in FauxPay.\n\n#### Impact\nA card token generated by the unauthenticated /tokenize endpoint is stored permanently and never removed after being consumed by /charge. Any party that possesses a previously-issued token can cause the API backend to initiate additional charges against that card without the cardholder re-presenting their credentials. In a real or partially-real deployment this translates to repeated debits from a card the customer presented only once.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker calls POST /fauxpay/tokenize with their own (or a stolen) card number and receives token T1. Attacker submits a checkout to the API backend using T1; the API backend calls /charge with T1 and transaction TX1 is created \u2014 T1 remains in the tokens Map. Attacker immediately submits a second checkout with the same T1 (because FauxPay has no single-use enforcement); the API backend calls /charge again with T1 and a second transaction TX2 is created against the same card. The attacker obtains two orders while the cardholder is charged twice, and the cycle can repeat indefinitely without the card ever being re-presented.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must know or be able to obtain a valid card_token (trivially satisfied for their own card; requires a second vulnerability such as IDOR or XSS to hijack another user's token)\n- The API backend must call /fauxpay/charge with the token (normal checkout flow satisfies this; the API key is held server-side and not directly attacker-controlled)\n\n```\n// line 38-39  \u2014 token stored with no expiry/use-count\nconst token = `tok_${crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex')}`;\ntokens.set(token, { last4: card_number.slice(-4), brand: detectBrand(card_number) });\n\n// line 45  \u2014 token looked up but NEVER deleted after use\nconst card = tokens.get(card_token);\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nFauxPay must enforce a 'consumed' state transition: after a successful charge, delete the token from the map (`tokens.delete(card_token)` at line 45 before the success response at line 54). Alternatively, implement a one-time-use flag (`tokens.set(token, { last4, brand, used: false })`) and reject any /charge request where `card.used === true`, setting it to `true` atomically before responding. This prevents any replay regardless of whether the API backend or SPA retains the token string.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 7.1. /fauxpay/tokenize requires no authentication; tokens persist indefinitely in the in-memory Map with no TTL or deletion after charge. Combined with finding 10 (hardcoded key), a pre-auth attacker can tokenize a card once and replay /charge calls unlimited times. Combined with finding 13 (duplicate checkout), a legitimate customer is double-billed from a\u2026"
