            "description": "#### Description\n`issueRefund` is an `async` function that performs two awaited network calls (lines 34 and 35). No loading/disabled state is set on the button at line 106 before the first `await`, and no flag is set to prevent re-entry. Because `setRefundAmount('')` and `setRefundReason('')` only execute after both awaits complete (or the catch branch), a second click while the first `api.csRefund` is outstanding will find the same `selected`, `refundAmount`, and `refundReason` values still in React state and will issue an identical second HTTP refund request.\n\n#### Impact\nThe 'Issue refund' button is never disabled while an async refund request is in-flight. A double-click (accidental or deliberate) fires two concurrent calls to `api.csRefund` with the identical `selected.id` and `amount_cents`. If the backend lacks idempotency controls, both refunds are processed, resulting in a customer receiving double the intended refund and a corresponding financial loss to the business.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nA CS agent opens an order and clicks 'Issue refund' twice in rapid succession (latency on the csRefund call gives a >200 ms window). Two concurrent POST requests hit the API with the same order ID and amount. If the Express/FauxPay layer processes both before any DB-level lock prevents it, the order receives two refunds. The second `openOrder` call (line 35) then refreshes the UI showing only one refund entry if the state is eventually consistent, masking the duplication.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker or careless user holds a Customer Service agent session\n- Network latency to the refund endpoint is large enough for a second click before the first request resolves (typical in a realistic deployment)\n\n```\nasync function issueRefund() {\n    setError('');\n    try {\n      const amount_cents = Math.round(Number(refundAmount) * 100);\n      await api.csRefund(selected.id, { amount_cents, reason: refundReason });\n      await openOrder(selected.id);\n      setRefundAmount('');\n      setRefundReason('');\n    } catch (err) {\n      setError(err.message);\n    }\n  }\n...\n<button onClick={issueRefund}>Issue refund</button>\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nAdd an `isSubmitting` state variable. Set it to `true` at the start of `issueRefund` and `false` in both the success and error paths. Bind `disabled={isSubmitting}` to the button at line 106. Additionally, the API endpoint should enforce idempotency (e.g., via a client-supplied idempotency key or a DB unique constraint on refund requests).\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 7.3. Requires CS-role auth (PR:L) and a timing race (AC:H), but scope changes (S:C) due to the cross-component FauxPay call. The button has no loading guard, so concurrent refund requests are trivially launched with a double-click or scripted parallel HTTP. The client-side race reliably widens the server-side TOCTOU window in finding 3. Together they produce n\u00d7 refund payouts from a single order.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 Frontend has no double-submit guard; the API's check-then-act is non-transactional; FauxPay's per-amount cap only blocks full-refund races, leaving partial-refund double-submit exploitable end-to-end with no defence in the path.\n\nI now have all the evidence needed. Let me summarize the full analysis:"
