            "description": "#### Description\nAt line 12, email is read from req.query. At line 14, the filter is only applied if email is truthy: `if (email) query = query.andWhereILike(...)`. An empty string (''), undefined, or omitted query parameter is falsy in JavaScript, so the WHERE clause is skipped entirely. The resulting Knex query at line 15 has no LIMIT and returns every row from the orders JOIN users table, ordered by created_at. This allows bulk extraction of customer PII (email, order totals, statuses) for every customer in the system through a single authenticated API call.\n\n#### Impact\nA customer_service user who sends GET /api/cs/orders with an absent or empty email parameter receives every order row in the database, including all customer email addresses, order totals, and statuses, with no row limit. A malicious or compromised CS agent can exfiltrate the entire customer order history in a single request.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker authenticates as a customer_service user (or compromises such an account). They call GET /api/cs/orders (no email parameter, or email=). The server executes SELECT orders.*, users.email AS customer_email FROM orders JOIN users ... ORDER BY orders.created_at DESC with no WHERE and no LIMIT, returning all rows. All customer emails and order histories are returned in one response, enabling wholesale exfiltration.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must hold a valid customer_service role session token\n- Attacker must call the API directly (or submit the search form with an empty email field)\n\n```\nlet query = db('orders').join('users', 'users.id', 'orders.user_id').select('orders.*', 'users.email as customer_email');\nif (email) query = query.andWhereILike('users.email', `%${email}%`);\nres.json(await query.orderBy('orders.created_at', 'desc'));\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nApply a mandatory LIMIT (e.g., 100\u2013500 rows) unconditionally at line 15: `query = query.limit(500)`. Additionally, require the email parameter to be non-empty before executing any query (return 400 if absent), so the endpoint cannot be used as a bulk dump mechanism. Also add an OFFSET-based or cursor-based pagination response envelope.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 6.5. Requires CS-role JWT (PR:L). Omitting or sending an empty email query parameter bypasses the WHERE clause entirely, returning every customer's order history with no LIMIT \u2014 a complete bulk PII exfiltration primitive in one API call. Becomes effectively pre-auth when chained with finding 1 (brute-force the seeded CS-agent default credentials).\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 The code does exactly what the scanner claims; no LIMIT exists, the email filter is silently skipped on empty/absent input, the route is network-accessible, and the only gate is a `customer_service` role check that does not prevent bulk dump.\n\nI now have everything I need. Let me summarize the findings:\n\n**Step A \u2014 Call graph edges**: The graph showed `andWhereILike` as the candidate function but no callers. The router middleware chain (`requireAuth \u2192 requireRole('customer_service')`) is the real gate, not captured in the graph. Verified in source.\n\n**Step B \u2014 What the code actually does** (cs.js, lines 11-16, confirmed):\n- `email` read from `req.query` (line 12)\n- Knex query built as `orders JOIN users` with `SELECT orders.*, users.email AS customer_email` \u2014 no LIMIT (line 13)\n- WHERE clause added **only if `email` is truthy** (line 14) \u2014 omitting the param or sending `email=` skips it entirely\n- Raw result returned (line 15)\n\nThis matches the scanner description exactly.\n\n**Step C \u2014 Entry point and auth chain** (auth.js, app.js confirmed):\n- Route is mounted at `POST /api/cs` \u2192 `GET /api/cs/orders` \u2014 reachable over the network\n- Router uses `router.use(requireAuth, requireRole('customer_service'))` \u2014 a real gatekeeper\n- `requireAuth` verifies a JWT signed with `JWT_SECRET`; `requireRole` checks `req.user.role === 'customer_service'`\n\nSo the entry point requires a valid `cus\u2026"
