            "description": "#### Description\nThe refund handler at lines 27\u201368 fetches the order (line 33) and checks only that (a) a payment record exists (line 35\u201336) and (b) the cumulative refund amount does not exceed `order.total_cents` (lines 38\u201342). It does not check `order.status`. Orders that have progressed through the exchange workflow retain their `payment_id` and have `refundedSoFar = 0` (no monetary refunds were issued, only an exchange), so both guards pass. The exchange workflow sets `order.status` to `exchange_pending` (cs.js:89) or `exchanged` (cs.js:108) but does not mark the payment as non-refundable. A CS agent can then call `POST /api/cs/orders/:id/refunds` on such an order, which succeeds: FauxPay issues the refund, a refunds row is inserted, and order status is overwritten to `refunded`, leaving an inconsistent DB state (exchange record + refund record on the same order) and double financial exposure.\n\n#### Impact\nA customer_service agent can issue a monetary refund against an order whose status is `exchange_pending` or `exchanged`, meaning the customer simultaneously receives a replacement item (via the exchange workflow) and a full cash refund. This results in the merchant issuing double compensation for a single order.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nCustomer reports a problem with order #99 (total_cents=5000). CS agent creates an exchange (order status \u2192 `exchange_pending`). Separately (or the same agent in a second browser tab), CS agent calls `POST /api/cs/orders/99/refunds` with `amount_cents: 5000`. The handler finds the payment, sees refundedSoFar=0, passes the guard, calls FauxPay, inserts the refund row, and sets order status to `refunded`. The customer now receives both the replacement widget and a full cash refund.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker holds a valid JWT with role=customer_service\n- Target order must have previously had an exchange created (status exchange_pending or exchanged) and have a payment_id set\n\n```\n  const order = await db('orders').where({ id: req.params.id }).first();\n  if (!order) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Order not found' });\n  // \u2190 NO status check here; exchange_pending / exchanged orders fall through\n  const payment = await db('payments').where({ id: order.payment_id }).first();\n  if (!payment) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Order has no associated payment' });\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nAdd an explicit order status guard at the start of the refund handler, before any DB or payment-processor operations: `if (!['paid', 'partially_refunded'].includes(order.status)) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Order is not in a refundable state' });`. Similarly, the exchange creation handler should reject orders already in `refunded` or `partially_refunded` state. These reciprocal guards prevent either path from overwriting the other's finalised outcome.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 6.5. Requires CS-role auth. The refund handler checks only payment existence and cumulative amount; it never inspects order.status. Exchanged orders retain a live payment_id and refundedSoFar = 0, so both guards pass. Standalone medium; escalates to high-value financial fraud when chained with findings 3 and 14 (TOCTOU + double-submit), enabling unlimited refunds on a single exchanged order.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 refund handler (lines 27\u201368) contains no `order.status` check; exchange workflow leaves `payment_id` intact with `refundedSoFar=0`, allowing any CS-role JWT holder to trigger double compensation on an exchanged order; verified in code with no countervailing guard found.\n\n**Analysis**\n\n**A. Call Graph Context**\nThe graph is sparse \u2014 it only identifies `andWhereILike` (the GET `/orders` search, line 14), not the refund or exchange handlers. I'll rely on direct code verification.\n\n**B. Actual Code at the Cited Lines**\n\n**Refund handler (lines 27\u201368) \u2014 confirmed exactly as the scanner described:**\n- Line 29\u201331: validates `amount_cents` is a positive integer \u2713\n- L\u2026"
