            "description": "#### Description\nThe refund cap logic reads the existing refund sum at line 38, evaluates the guard at line 40, then performs an external async network call to FauxPay at line 46, and only then inserts the refund row into the DB at lines 51\u201359. There is no database transaction, row-level lock, or SELECT FOR UPDATE wrapping this window. Because the FauxPay call is an awaited async HTTP request (potentially hundreds of milliseconds), the race window is wide. Two concurrent POST /api/cs/orders/:id/refunds requests with amount_cents = order.total_cents will both read refundedSoFar = 0, both satisfy the guard (0 + total_cents <= total_cents), both call fauxpay.refund(), and both insert a row, producing total refunded = 2 \u00d7 order.total_cents.\n\n#### Impact\nTwo concurrent CS-role POST requests to the same order's refund endpoint can both pass the cap check before either inserts its refund row, causing the processor (FauxPay) to execute two full-amount refunds totalling 2\u00d7 the order charge. Each successful race yields financial loss equal to one additional order total. Any holder of a customer_service session token can trigger this repeatedly.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAttacker holds a customer_service JWT. They identify order #42 with total_cents = 5000. They fire two simultaneous POST /api/cs/orders/42/refunds requests with body {amount_cents:5000, reason:'test'}. Both requests read alreadyRefunded.total = 0 at line 38, both pass the guard at line 40 (0+5000 <= 5000), both await fauxpay.refund() concurrently, and both successfully insert a refund row. FauxPay processes two $50 credits. The merchant suffers a $50 unrecoverable loss. Repeating across many orders causes unbounded financial damage.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must hold a valid customer_service role session token (requireRole enforced at line 9)\n\n```\nconst alreadyRefunded = await db('refunds').where({ order_id: order.id }).sum('amount_cents as total').first();\nconst refundedSoFar = Number(alreadyRefunded.total || 0);\nif (refundedSoFar + amount_cents > order.total_cents) {\n  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Refund amount exceeds order total' });\n}\n\nlet result;\ntry {\n  result = await fauxpay.refund({ transactionId: payment.processor_transaction_id, amountCents: amount_cents });\n} catch (err) {\n  return res.status(502).json({ error: 'Refund failed at processor', detail: err.data?.error });\n}\n\nconst [row] = await db('refunds')\n  .insert({ order_id: order.id, payment_id: payment.id, issued_by: req.user.sub, amount_cents, reason, processor_refund_id: result.refund_id })\n  .returning('id');\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nWrap lines 38\u201359 in a database transaction with a pessimistic row-level lock on the order row (e.g., Knex .forUpdate() on the orders SELECT at line 33) so that concurrent requests serialize. Alternatively, insert the refund row with a DB-level constraint first (before calling FauxPay) and roll back on processor failure. The critical invariant is: the cap read and the refund record write must be atomic.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 6.3. Requires CS-role auth and a race win (AC:H). The async FauxPay HTTP call creates a 100-500 ms window between the refund cap check and the DB insert, with no SELECT FOR UPDATE or transaction wrapping. Finding 14 provides the reliable concurrent-request mechanism to win this race. Finding 5 extends the exploitable surface to previously exchanged orders.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 Code exactly matches the scanner's description; no DB transaction, no locking, no unique constraint, and a wide async race window between the non-locking SUM guard and the DB insert allow two concurrent requests to each refund the full order amount.\n\nI have now read all relevant source files. Let me assess the finding against the actual code."
