            "description": "#### Description\nAt api/src/routes/auth.js:43, when `db('users').where({ email }).first()` returns null (email not registered), the condition `!user` short-circuits the OR and bcrypt.compare is never called. When the email exists but the password is wrong, bcrypt.compare runs for ~50\u2013100 ms (cost factor 10). This creates a measurable ~50\u2013100 ms timing difference between the two error paths that return the same HTTP 401 and identical JSON body. An attacker sending repeated requests with the same wrong password can binary-search the user table: emails returning in ~2\u20135 ms are unregistered; emails returning in ~60 ms are registered.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker can distinguish between 'email does not exist' and 'email exists but wrong password' via response timing, enabling enumeration of registered email addresses. This converts the generic error message into a user enumeration oracle, accelerating targeted credential attacks or phishing campaigns against confirmed accounts.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker sends POST /api/auth/login with {\"email\":\"target@example.com\",\"password\":\"[REDACTED-SECRET]\"} 10 times and measures average response time. If avg < 10 ms the email is not registered; if avg > 50 ms the email is registered. By repeating across a list of candidate emails the attacker builds a confirmed-active-account list for subsequent credential stuffing. No authentication or special access is required.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Network access to the public login endpoint\n- Ability to measure HTTP response latency (standard for any HTTP client)\n\n```\n  const user = await db('users').where({ email }).first();\n  if (!user || !(await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password_hash))) {\n    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid email or password' });\n  }\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nWhen the user is not found, perform a dummy bcrypt.compare against a constant hash to equalise timing between both error paths. Example: in api/src/routes/auth.js after line 43, if `!user`, call `await bcrypt.compare(password, '$2a$10$dummyhashvaluethatnevermatchesXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX')` before returning 401. This ensures both paths take the same ~60\u2013100 ms and eliminates the timing oracle.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 5.3 (C:L). Pre-auth. The ~50-100 ms timing difference between absent-user and wrong-password paths is measurable over low-latency paths. Standalone value is limited to email existence enumeration. Primary chain value is as a reconnaissance primitive feeding finding 1's brute-force with a validated email list, reducing the attack space to confirmed accounts.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 bcrypt.compare is conditionally skipped for unknown emails; no rate limiting or constant-time dummy compare exists to neutralise the ~60 ms timing gap on a public unauthenticated endpoint\n\n**Analysis complete.** Here is my full verification chain:\n\n**Step B \u2014 Verify the cited code:**\nThe file exists at the exact path. Lines 43\u201346 match the scanner's snippet precisely:\n- `db('users').where({ email }).first()` is the DB lookup (fast, ~2\u20135 ms)\n- `if (!user || !(await bcrypt.compare(\u2026)))` \u2014 short-circuit means `bcrypt.compare` is **never called** when `!user` is true\n- Both branches return identical HTTP 401 + `{ error: 'Invalid email or password' }`\n- Cost factor used at registration (line 25): `bcrypt.hash(password, 10)` \u2192 ~60\u2013100 ms per compare\n\n**Step C \u2014 Walk the entry point:**\n- `app.js` line 22: `app.use('/api/auth', authRoutes)` \u2014 the login route is mounted directly with **no middleware applied before it**\n- `server.js`: straight `app.listen` \u2014 no reverse-proxy rate-limiting in the application layer\n\n**Step D \u2014 Search for defences:**\n- No `express-rate-limit`, `helmet`, IP throttling, or any rate-limiting package referenced in `app.js` or `server.js`\n- `cors()` is invoked with **no origin restrictions** (line 16, `app.js`) \u2014 cross-origin requests allowed\n- No dummy/constant-time hash comparison to normalise timing when u\u2026"
