            "description": "#### Description\nThe `submit` async handler (Checkout.jsx:33) never inspects the current value of `submitting` before proceeding. The guard is entirely UI-side: `setSubmitting(true)` schedules a React state update which only disables the button after the next render flush. In React 18, that flush happens when the async handler yields at its first `await` (line 39, `await tokenizeCard(card)`), meaning the button is not yet `disabled` in the DOM during the synchronous preamble of `submit`. If a second form-submit event is already queued in the browser's event loop before that flush fires \u2014 reachable via rapid double-click on the Pay button, two rapid Enter-key presses in a form field, or any programmatic `form.submit()` call \u2014 a second invocation of `submit` executes concurrently with `submitting === false` in its closure (because it was captured from the pre-flush render). Both invocations pass the `!addressId` check (line 38), both call `tokenizeCard`, and both call `api.checkout` (line 40), creating two orders. The trust boundary crossed is: external user-driven form submission event (untrusted input) \u2192 `api.checkout` call that authorises a financial transaction (security decision).\n\n#### Impact\nAn authenticated user can submit the checkout form twice concurrently before React's re-render propagates the `submitting=true` disabled state to the button, causing two separate `api.checkout()` calls and therefore two orders being placed and two card charges. Any authenticated shopper is affected; the impact is financial (double charge) and inventory (double deduction).\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn authenticated user with items in the cart and a valid address navigates to /checkout, fills in card details, and double-clicks the 'Pay' button rapidly (or holds Enter in a card input field). Both click/submit events are queued before React's state flush runs. The first `submit()` call executes synchronously through `setSubmitting(true)` and suspends at `await tokenizeCard`; the second `submit()` call begins with the stale `submitting=false` closure value, also passes the `!addressId` guard, calls `tokenizeCard` again, and \u2014 when both awaits resolve \u2014 both independently call `api.checkout`, placing two identical orders and charging the user's card twice.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- User is authenticated with a valid session\n- User has items in the cart and at least one shipping address\n- User can trigger two form-submit events before the React re-render disables the button (double-click, rapid Enter keypresses, or any script that calls form.submit())\n\n```\nasync function submit(e) {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    setError('');\n    setSubmitting(true);      // schedules state update \u2013 button NOT yet disabled in DOM\n    try {\n      if (!addressId) throw new Error('Please select or add a shipping address');\n      const card_token = await tokenizeCard(card);   // first await; re-render flushes HERE\n      const order = await api.checkout({ shipping_address_id: Number(addressId), card_token });\n      navigate(`/orders`, { state: { justPlaced: order.id } });\n    } catch (err) {\n      setError(err.message);\n    } finally {\n      setSubmitting(false);\n    }\n  }\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nAdd an explicit re-entrancy guard at the top of the `submit` handler: `if (submitting) return;`. Using a React ref (`const submittingRef = useRef(false)`) instead of (or in addition to) state gives a synchronous, closure-stable guard that is not subject to render-cycle timing: set `submittingRef.current = true` before the first `await` and reset it in `finally`. The UI `disabled={submitting}` state should remain for UX but must not be the sole protection against concurrent invocations.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 3.5. Requires auth (PR:L) and UI interaction (UI:R \u2014 rapid double-click or queued form-submit events). React 18's render-flush timing creates a genuine race but requires a specific interaction pattern. Standalone I:L (duplicate order). Chained with finding 15 (non-invalidated\u2026"
