            "description": "#### Description\nThe stock-sufficiency check at lines 33\u201339 reads widget.stock_quantity from a snapshot fetched at line 30, entirely outside the database transaction that begins at line 47. Inside the transaction (lines 61\u201362), stock is decremented with a plain knex .decrement() \u2014 equivalent to UPDATE widgets SET stock_quantity = stock_quantity - N WHERE id = X \u2014 without any WHERE stock_quantity >= N guard. Two concurrent requests each read the pre-decrement snapshot, each determine stock is sufficient, each enter their own transaction, and each decrement. The second decrement brings stock_quantity below zero. There is no floor in the SQL, no SELECT FOR UPDATE on the widgets row, and no post-decrement verification.\n\n#### Impact\nTwo authenticated users (or one user with two concurrent browser tabs) checking out the same low-stock widget simultaneously can both pass the stock guard and both have their orders committed, driving stock_quantity negative. The merchant ships items it does not have. A deliberate attacker with two accounts and one remaining unit of a product can reliably trigger this.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nWidget #7 has stock_quantity=1. Attacker controls two user accounts. Both submit POST /api/orders simultaneously with widget #7 in their carts (quantity=1). Both requests fetch stock_quantity=1 at line 30. Both pass the check at line 36 (1 >= 1). Both open DB transactions; the first commits the decrement (stock\u21920), the second commits its decrement (stock\u2192-1). Both charges succeed through fauxpay. Both orders are recorded as 'paid'. The merchant has sold one physical widget to two customers.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Two independently authenticated user sessions (any role \u2014 standard customers suffice)\n- A widget with stock_quantity <= the quantity in each cart at the moment of concurrent checkout\n\n```\n// CHECK is outside the transaction (lines 33-39)\nfor (const item of cartItems) {\n  const widget = widgetsById.get(item.widget_id);\n  if (widget.stock_quantity < item.quantity) {\n    return res.status(400).json({ error: `Insufficient stock for ${widget.name}` });\n  }\n}\n\n// DECREMENT is inside the transaction (lines 61-62) \u2014 no re-check, no floor\nfor (const li of lineItems) {\n  await trx('widgets').where({ id: li.widget_id }).decrement('stock_quantity', li.quantity);\n}\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nMove the stock check inside the transaction and use a conditional atomic decrement: UPDATE widgets SET stock_quantity = stock_quantity - N WHERE id = X AND stock_quantity >= N, then check the affected-row count. If zero rows updated, roll back and return an out-of-stock error. Alternatively, add a CHECK constraint (stock_quantity >= 0) at the DB level as a backstop so the second commit fails rather than corrupting inventory.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 3.1. Requires auth (PR:L) and a concurrent race win (AC:H). The stock check runs entirely outside the transaction; the in-transaction decrement has no WHERE stock_quantity >= N floor. Standalone I:L (oversell by one unit per race win). Amplified by finding 8 \u2014 concurrent requests that also fail payment leave stock_quantity permanently negative, compounding the inventory corruption.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 TOCTOU confirmed: stock read and checked outside the transaction, decremented inside with no re-check and no DB floor; any two authenticated customers can concurrently oversell a widget to negative stock\n\n**Analysis Complete**\n\n**Step A\u2013B: Verify the cited code**\n\nThe actual file (`orders.js`, lines 11\u201394) matches the scanner's claim precisely:\n- **Line 30**: `db('widgets').whereIn('id', widgetIds).andWhere({ is_active: true })` \u2014 a plain `SELECT`, no lock, executed **outside** any transaction.\n- **Lines 33\u201339**: Stock-sufficiency check (`widget.stock_quantity < item.quantity`) runs against that stale snapshot, also outside the transaction.\n- **Line 47**: `db.transaction(async (trx) => { ... })` \u2014 transaction opens here, *after* the check\u2026"
