            "description": "#### Description\n`updatePrice` reads a price string from `window.prompt`, converts it with `Math.round(Number(priceStr) * 100)`, then checks `!Number.isInteger(price_cents) || price_cents < 0`. `Math.round()` always returns an integer for any finite input, so the `Number.isInteger` arm never fires for valid numerics. The `< 0` guard rejects negatives but allows `price_cents === 0` (input string `\"0\"` or empty string \u2014 `Number(\"\") * 100 = 0`). The zero value is forwarded directly to `api.adminUpdateWidget(widget.id, { price_cents })` at line 39 with no further constraint.\n\n#### Impact\nAn admin (or attacker with admin credentials) can set any widget's price to $0.00, allowing any shopper to place orders at no cost. Because the validation only blocks strictly-negative values (`price_cents < 0`), zero passes through and is sent to the update endpoint.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker with admin access opens the Admin console, clicks 'Set price' for a high-value widget, and enters \"0\" in the prompt. `price_cents` evaluates to `0`, passes the guard, and the API call sets the widget price to $0.00. Any customer can then add the item to their cart and complete checkout for free until the price is manually corrected.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker must hold an admin-role session\n\n```\nconst price_cents = Math.round(Number(priceStr) * 100);\nif (!Number.isInteger(price_cents) || price_cents < 0) return;\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nChange the guard at line 37 to `price_cents <= 0` (or enforce a minimum such as `price_cents < 1`) to reject zero as well as negative prices: `if (!Number.isFinite(price_cents) || price_cents <= 0) return;`. Also validate on the server side in the admin widget-update route.\n\n**Exploitability:** CVSS 2.7. Requires admin role (PR:H). Math.round() returns an integer for any finite input so the isInteger check never fires for valid numerics; the < 0 guard misses zero. If the API layer independently validates price_cents > 0, this is informational. If not, an admin (compromised via finding 1) can set zero-dollar prices, enabling free purchases for any customer. Standalone I:L.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 zero price passes both the frontend guard (`price_cents < 0`) and the identical server-side guard in `PATCH /admin/widgets/:id`; the DB write proceeds, enabling free checkout for all customers; impact confirmed end-to-end.\n\nI now have all the information needed. Let me analyze the complete picture:\n\n**Frontend (Admin.jsx lines 33-44) \u2014 confirmed:**\n- `Math.round(Number(\"0\") * 100)` \u2192 `price_cents = 0`\n- Guard: `!Number.isInteger(0) || 0 < 0` \u2192 `false || false` \u2192 **guard does not fire**\n- `0` is forwarded to `api.adminUpdateWidget`\n\n**Backend (api/src/routes/admin.js lines 39-43) \u2014 verified:**\n```js\nif (!Number.isInteger(updates.price_cents) || updates.price_cents < 0) {\n  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'price_cents must be a non-negative integer' });\n}\n```\nIdentical guard logic. `price_cents = 0` satisfies `Number.isInteger(0) === true` and `0 < 0 === false`, so **the backend also accepts zero**. The DB write proceeds.\n\n**Auth gate (admin.js line 8):** `router.use(requireAuth, requireRole('admin'))` \u2014 all admin routes require a valid admin-role session. This is a real external HTTP entry point, but locked behind PR:H.\n\n**No further defence found:** no DB-level constraint requiring `price_cents > 0`, no checkout-side re-validation against a minimum price.\n\n**Summary:** The scanner's technical description is fully accurate. The `< 0` guard was written to block negatives but was never extended to block zero. The same gap exists identically on both the frontend and the backend API handler. An admin (including a compromised admin account) can drive any widget's price to $0, making it freely purchasable by all customers until manually corrected. The only barrier is obtaining admin credentials \u2014 which are subject to phishing, session hijacking, or insider threat."
